/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  constant/economics -- the prices this appraisal is priced at (DEV.md 4c C44,
  slice 4; the three prices replaced by CITED public figures on 2026-11-13).
  Currency: EUR per kg of the stream named.

  EVERY PRICE BELOW IS A PUBLIC, DATED, CITED FIGURE, read from the document
  its provenance block names, with the arithmetic and the exchange rate
  written beside it.  They are three DIFFERENT KINDS of number and the file
  says which each is:
    * ProductNH3 is the EU's IMPORT UNIT VALUE of anhydrous ammonia in 2025
      (a customs value at the EU border), used here as the market price of
      CONVENTIONAL (fossil) ammonia; not a price at the gate, not a contract
      price.  No transaction or contract price of renewable
      ammonia could be read, so none is assumed: the premium this plant needs
      is not an input, it is what the engine's break-even prints
      (`breakEven_price_ProductNH3` in system/postDict);
    * H2Feed is the regulator's own statement of the average COST of
      renewable (RFNBO) hydrogen in the EU, with its range -- a production
      cost estimate current in October 2025.  The source states no delivered
      price and no pressure: that the plant pays this cost at its fence is
      THE CASE'S READING, not the source's;
    * N2Feed is a MODELLED nitrogen PRODUCTION COST (a paper's table), not a
      merchant price.  It is about 1 % of the raw-material bill here.
  The ranges the sources span (low and high, each cited) feed the
  `sensitivity {}` block of the two feasibility cases; they are in the
  provenance `method` of each price below.

  WHAT IS PRICED HERE AND WHAT IS NOT.
    * the two RAW MATERIALS that cross the battery limit: electrolytic
      hydrogen (H2Feed) and ASU nitrogen (N2Feed).  The electrolyser and the
      ASU are outside this battery limit, so their electricity and capital
      are inside these two prices, not in this plant's utility bill;
    * the PRODUCT, liquid ammonia (ProductNH3);
    * operating labour.
    The UTILITIES (the compressors' electricity, cooling water, the ammonia
    refrigeration and the let-down steam) are NOT priced here: the utility
    allocation prices each duty from its record in data/standards/utilities/,
    and the economics pass reads that bill.  The StorageFlash duty no
    utility in the catalogue can serve is announced on the run as unpriced,
    and the utility bill is then a LOWER BOUND, said on the run.
    The purge (Purge) and the flash gases (LetdownGas, FlashGas) carry no
    price: their hydrogen is lost, which the H2 price already charges, and
    no credit is taken for them as fuel.

  AT THESE PRICES THE PLANT LOSES MONEY, and that is the finding, not a
  defect: hydrogen at 8 EUR/kg alone costs 0.1803 x 8 = 1.44 EUR per kg of
  ammonia made, against a market price of 0.53.  The labour rate below is
  still the one figure in this file that is an assumption (see it).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
products
(
    {
        stream    ProductNH3;
        price     0.530;              // EUR/kg NH3 -- Eurostat, EU import unit value of anhydrous ammonia, 2025
        costYear  2025;
        provenance
        {
            origin  "Eurostat, Comext database, dataset DS-045409 'EU trade since 1988 by HS2-4-6 and CN8', product CN 28141000 'Anhydrous ammonia', flow IMPORT, reporter EU27, partner extra-EU27, calendar year 2025: value 1 427 171 639 EUR, quantity 2 693 549 t (26 935 494.25 x 100 kg); all twelve months of 2025 are in the data, and the dataset does not state whether the year is final; retrieved 2026-11-13 (dataset updated 2026-11-13) from https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/api/comext/dissemination/statistics/1.0/data/DS-045409.  This is the EU's IMPORT UNIT VALUE (a customs value at the EU border), used here as the market price of CONVENTIONAL ammonia; it is not a price at the gate, not a contract price, and not a price of renewable ammonia";
            method  "unit value = value / quantity = 1 427 171 639 EUR / 2 693 549 000 kg = 0.52985 EUR/kg, rounded to 0.530; already in EUR, no exchange rate.  RANGE READ (the sensitivity ends): low 0.439 EUR/kg = USGS, Mineral Commodity Summaries, Nitrogen (fixed) - ammonia, February 2026, p. 1, average price free on board Gulf Coast 2025, 450 USD per short ton / 907.185 kg = 0.4960 USD/kg / 1.12998 USD per EUR (ECB euro reference rate, annual average 2025, https://data-api.ecb.europa.eu/service/data/EXR/A.USD.EUR.SP00.A, retrieved 2026-11-13); high 0.726 EUR/kg = the same Eurostat dataset, January-September 2026 (provisional), value / quantity over the nine months";
        }
    }
);
rawMaterials
(
    {
        stream    H2Feed;             // renewable (RFNBO) electrolytic H2 at 30 bar
        price     8.0;                // EUR/kg H2 -- ACER, average COST of RFNBO hydrogen in the EU, October 2025
        costYear  2025;
        provenance
        {
            origin  "ACER (EU Agency for the Cooperation of Energy Regulators), 'European hydrogen markets, 2025 Monitoring Report', 2 December 2025, pdf p. 40 (para 78): 'Notably, the average cost of RFNBO hydrogen remains significantly higher at around 8 EUR/kg, ranging between 7-10 EUR/kg (purple area in the graph), depending on the country of origin' (an estimate current in October 2025, which the report bases on cost estimates of a commercial data provider, S&P Global Commodity Insights); the same figure is key finding 5, p. 5: 'At around 8 EUR/kgH2, the average cost of RFNBO hydrogen in the EU'; https://www.acer.europa.eu/sites/default/files/documents/Publications/ACER-2025-European-hydrogen-markets.pdf, retrieved 2026-11-13";
            method  "taken as published, in EUR/kg, no conversion.  The source states a COST, and no delivered price and no pressure; THIS CASE reads that cost as what the plant pays at its fence for hydrogen at 30 bar -- the case's reading, not the source's: compression to 30 bar and a supplier's margin are not in the source's figure.  RANGE READ (the sensitivity ends): high 10.0 EUR/kg = the top of the report's 7-10 range; low 4.95 EUR/kg = the same report, p. 26, the average price ammonia producers reported they would pay for renewable hydrogen in the second European Hydrogen Bank auction ('4.95 EUR/kg'), a stated willingness to pay, not a transaction.  CROSS-CHECKS READ: 6.17 EUR/kg (European Hydrogen Observatory, 'European average for renewable hydrogen in 2025', https://observatory.clean-hydrogen.europa.eu/, page undated) and about 6 EUR/kg (MIBGAS IBHYX index, January-October 2025, as quoted by ACER p. 41)";
        }
    }
    {
        stream    N2Feed;             // ASU nitrogen, 99.99 % N2, 100 ppm Ar
        price     0.0175;             // EUR/kg N2 -- OIES, modelled nitrogen PRODUCTION COST
        costYear  2024;
        provenance
        {
            origin  "Oxford Institute for Energy Studies, OIES Paper ET40 'Fueling the future: a techno-economic evaluation of e-ammonia production for marine applications' (N. Souissi), October 2024, Table 2 'Relevant costs data' (pdf p. 25), row 'Nitrogen production costs per ton', columns 'Present', six regions: 18.98 (Chile Atacama, PV), 17.68 (Chile Patagonia, wind onshore), 18.87 (Australia, PV), 17.9 (United States, wind onshore), 18.39 (Canada, wind onshore), 17.25 (Middle East, PV), in USD per tonne; https://www.oxfordenergy.org/wpcms/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/ET40-Fuelling-the-future-final.pdf, retrieved 2026-11-13.  A MODELLED production COST of nitrogen, not a price";
            method  "the highest of the six 'Present' values, Chile (Atacama) PV, 18.98 USD/t / 1000 / 1.08238 USD per EUR (ECB euro reference rate, annual average 2024, the year of the paper, https://data-api.ecb.europa.eu/service/data/EXR/A.USD.EUR.SP00.A, retrieved 2026-11-13) = 0.01754 EUR/kg, rounded to 0.0175; the only nitrogen figure that could be read.  RANGE READ (the sensitivity ends): the table's 'Present' range, low 17.25 USD/t = 0.01594 EUR/kg (15.94 EUR/t), high 18.98 USD/t = 0.01754 EUR/kg (17.54 EUR/t)";
        }
    }
);
labourRate      42.0;                 // EUR/h loaded operating labour -- the
                                      // figure ammonia02 uses, an assumption
labourRateYear  2026;
